"""Author: Sandeep Belgavi. Original interactive lessons for AICassindra."""
import html
import json

LESSONS = [
    ('architecture', '01 / Architecture repair', 'Stop the action before it escapes.',
     {'steps':['identity','model','budget','minimize','approval','action','validate','deliver','audit']},
     'A support agent drafts a refund and immediately invokes its payment tool. Its prompt says to be careful, but the gateway checks the budget only after generation and records the audit after delivery. Your job is to repair the prerequisite order. Edit the steps array; retain all nine stages exactly once. Run the fixture and use each failed edge to locate the misplaced control.',
     'First move budget and minimize before model. Put validation after generation, then approval before action. Audit the outcome before delivering it. The identity stage represents an authenticated principal and authorized scope, not a name supplied by the browser. In a deployed service, policy enforcement belongs in trusted code and must run for every invocation, including retries and tool callbacks.',
     'A green result proves only that this small graph respects its declared dependencies. It cannot prove that a human actually approved the exact action, that an audit write is durable, or that the output validator understands the business rule. Bind approval to an immutable action digest and principal; expire it; reject replay; and record both attempted effects and their outcomes. An audit outage after a completed effect requires reconciliation, not silently repeating the effect.',
     'Hard mode: place audit before action and explain why a record of intent cannot stand in for a record of the outcome. Then design a second audit event before the effect. This exercise contains one audit stage for the delivered result; a production design normally needs both events and an idempotency key.',
     ['03-hybrid-agent-architecture','06-afp-and-prompt-injection']),
    ('contract', '02 / Decision forensics', 'Valid JSON can still be a bad decision.',
     {'choice':'proceed','probabilities':{'proceed':0.7,'review':0.4,'block':0.1}},
     'The provider returned a syntactically correct object, but the distribution adds up to more than one. Inspect the response as a gateway would. Repair the three probabilities, then deliberately select a non-maximum option. The validator should reject that mismatch. The exercise checks a narrow Choice-style contract, not every field in a real provider response.',
     'Try proceed at 0.90, review at 0.08 and block at 0.02. The result waits for approval; it never executes an action. Next reduce proceed to 0.80 and raise review to 0.18. The same selected label now enters review because this fixture applies a threshold of 0.85. That number is an explicit teaching policy, not a claim that the model is calibrated or safe for refunds.',
     'The most important experiment uses a structurally valid distribution whose selected answer you know is wrong. The contract accepts the shape because it cannot know the ground truth. Evaluate correctness separately on labeled examples, report false positives and missed positives, and examine results by scenario. Do not turn confidence into permission or silently reinterpret an unfamiliar option as a familiar action.',
     'Hard mode: add an execute_refund option, use a string instead of a number, or create a tied maximum. Unknown options and incorrect types fail. A tied maximum is structurally allowed here but should trigger a separate policy decision in a real workflow. Write that policy before changing the validator, so evaluation measures intended behavior.',
     ['02-typed-decision-contract','04-confidence-calibration']),
    ('minimizer', '03 / Evidence boundary', 'Keep the secret out of the worker.',
     {'order_status':'Delivered yesterday','refund_rule':'Review unopened items within 14 days','email':'synthetic@example.invalid','payment_token':'FAKE-DO-NOT-USE'},
     'A refund reviewer needs an order fact and the applicable refund rule. The surrounding customer record contains unrelated fields. Run the fixture and inspect the exact simulated worker state. Only order_status and refund_rule survive. Nothing is sent to a model or logged to a server. Drafts are saved on this device until you clear them. Use synthetic values while experimenting.',
     'Remove the refund_rule field and run again. The minimizer fails closed instead of inventing a default policy. Empty strings and evidence longer than 4096 characters are also rejected. This is a field allowlist: it makes the input contract explicit and reduces accidental disclosure from broad object serialization. The omitted field count is shown without echoing the dropped values in the result.',
     'Now place a fake secret inside order_status. It remains in the output because field selection is not semantic redaction. This counterexample matters: calling an object minimized does not mean it contains no personal data. Production inputs need a purpose-specific schema, provenance, applicable retention controls, and a deliberate policy for free text. Detection of sensitive content is a separate control with its own false positives and misses.',
     'Hard mode: insert an instruction such as ignore policy inside the order text. It remains untrusted evidence, not authorization. The browser fixture does not execute that instruction and does not measure whether a real model would follow it. Add adversarial documents to a controlled evaluation, then verify that tool permissions remain bounded even if the model produces a harmful recommendation.',
     ['06-afp-and-prompt-injection','09-rag-evidence-verification']),
    ('budget', '04 / Retry economics', 'Who pays for the failed attempt?',
     {'remaining':2,'requests':[{'authorized':False,'providerFails':False},{'authorized':True,'providerFails':True},{'authorized':True,'providerFails':False},{'authorized':True,'providerFails':False}]},
     'An agent has two remaining calls and a queue of four requests. One caller lacks authorization and one provider attempt fails. Predict the call count before running the simulation. Unauthorized requests are denied without consuming a reservation. Authorized attempts reserve a call before provider work, and the failure does not automatically refund it. The last request should exhaust the budget.',
     'Change the initial budget to zero and then to three. Compare the trace rather than looking only at the final number. This lab models call reservations, not currency, tokens, rate limits, or vendor billing. A real cost guard needs estimated upper bounds, measured usage reconciliation, per-principal limits, and an explicit policy for provider timeouts whose billing outcome may be unknown.',
     'The sequence is deterministic and runs in one browser task. It does not demonstrate atomic enforcement under concurrency. In a deployed gateway, two workers reading the same remaining count can both believe they have permission. Use an atomic reservation in the authoritative ledger and make the reservation identity stable across retries. Never trust the remaining count supplied by a client.',
     'Hard mode: explain why blindly retrying the failed call can exceed the budget even though no response was delivered. Then design bounded retries with a deadline and reserved capacity. A monetary refund from a provider and a release of application capacity are separate events; do not conflate them. The output intentionally distinguishes denied, exhausted, failed and received attempts.',
     ['08-model-routing-and-cost','12-gcp-realtime-deployment']),
    ('partition', '05 / Tool boundary', 'Build a query plan without opening a database.',
     {'table':'demo.events','partition':{'tenant_id':'tenant-demo'},'columns':['event_id','status'],'limit':20},
     'This miniature schema exposes one table with a single partition key. Supply the full tenant_id key, choose explicit columns, and keep the limit between one and one hundred. The output is a prepared query plan with separate parameters. It never connects to Cassandra. The companion Java, Python and Node implementations perform real schema-aware checks; this lesson uses a fixed teaching schema.',
     'Replace the table with system.local, add an unknown column, repeat a column, or request a limit of 101. Each should fail before a plan is shown. Change the tenant value to a different valid identifier and observe that a plan is still produced. That is a deliberate demonstration of a missing layer: this tool-level schema check is not an authenticated tenant authorization system.',
     'Prepared values prevent a value from becoming query syntax, while allowed identifiers prevent table and column names from being chosen freely. Neither protection grants a user permission to see a partition. The trusted gateway must derive tenant scope from verified identity and compare the requested key against it. The database role should remain SELECT-only and limited to the exact tables needed.',
     'Hard mode: explain why a small row limit is not a complete memory limit when one row contains a huge cell. The real servers apply response bounds but do not claim to prevent all driver-side allocations. Use bounded schemas and production data tests. This fixture validates a restricted string key; it does not claim compatibility with every Cassandra type or composite-key arrangement.',
     ['02-typed-decision-contract','10-evaluation-and-fault-testing']),
]

from harness_lessons import HARNESS_LESSONS
LESSONS.extend(HARNESS_LESSONS)

def render():
    e = html.escape
    result = '<section><div class="wrap"><div class="notice">Eight original browser simulations by Sandeep Belgavi. No login, API key, model download or payment is needed. Drafts stay on this device; no inference, database access, business action or deployment occurs. Results are fixture behavior, not a security certification.</div><nav class="lab-jump" aria-label="Exercises">'
    result += ''.join(f'<a href="#{kind}">{e(label)}</a>' for kind,label,*_ in LESSONS)
    result += '</nav><div id="mission-dashboard" class="debrief"><h2>Your next useful step</h2><p id="progress-summary" aria-live="polite">Start your first mission.</p><progress id="mission-progress" max="24" value="0" aria-label="Completed mission checkpoints"></progress><p><a class="button" id="resume-mission" href="#architecture">Start mission 1</a> <a id="weekly-mission" href="#architecture">Weekly practice</a></p><p id="storage-status">Progress and drafts save on this browser. Use synthetic data only. Clearing browser storage removes them; there is no account sync.</p><label><input type="checkbox" id="share-metrics"> Allow Google Analytics to measure mission usage (uses analytics cookies; no JSON inputs or results)</label><div class="toolbar"><button id="export-progress" type="button">Download progress report</button><button id="clear-progress" type="button">Clear saved progress</button></div><details><summary>My activity trail</summary><p>Only the latest 200 events are retained on this device. These records are editable local practice records, not verified credentials.</p><ol id="activity-trail"></ol></details></div></div></section>'
    for kind,label,title,fixture,opening,instructions,meaning,hard,articles in LESSONS:
        result += f'''<section id="{kind}" class="exercise" data-exercise="{kind}"><div class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow">{e(label)}</div><h2>{e(title)}</h2><p>{e(opening)}</p><details class="lesson-hint"><summary>Read the guided walkthrough</summary><p>{e(instructions)}</p></details><div class="mission-controls"></div><div class="workspace"><div><label for="input-{kind}">Editable JSON fixture</label><textarea id="input-{kind}" spellcheck="false">{e(json.dumps(fixture,indent=2))}</textarea><div class="toolbar"><button data-run type="button">Run experiment</button><button data-reset type="button">Reset fixture</button></div></div><div><span data-status>Ready</span><output aria-live="polite" aria-label="{e(title)} result">Run the fixture to inspect the result.</output></div></div><div class="debrief"><h3>What the result establishes</h3><p>{e(meaning)}</p><p>{e(hard)}</p></div><p>Read the reasoning: '''
        result += ' · '.join(f'<a href="/blogs/decision_systems/{slug}.html">{e(slug[3:].replace("-"," ").capitalize())}</a>' for slug in articles)
        if kind in ('replay','promotion','outcome'):
            result += ' <a href="/blogs/harness_engineering/index.html">Read the harness engineering series</a>'
        result += '</p></div></section>'
    return result + '<script defer src="/mcp/decision-labs.js"></script><script defer src="/mcp/harness-models.js"></script><script defer src="/mcp/mission-path.js"></script><noscript><p>JavaScript is required to run these experiments. The lessons remain readable without it.</p></noscript>'
